#!/bin/sh
# post-commit -- notice the commits that never ran pre-commit, and say the pages they owe.
#
# WHY THIS HOOK EXISTS. tools/hooks/pre-commit refreshes the two generated front-door pages --
# README.md and docs-geode/libraries/README.md -- inside the commit that moves their numbers. It
# does that for `git commit` and for `git commit --amend`, and for nothing else, because git runs
# pre-commit for those two paths alone. Measured on real repositories in a throwaway pen
# (tools/fixtures/g/generated_page_freshness_control.sh, cases 9 and 10):
#
#   git commit           pre-commit fires    post-commit fires
#   git commit --amend   pre-commit fires    post-commit fires
#   git cherry-pick      SKIPPED             post-commit fires
#   git rebase           SKIPPED             post-commit fires
#   git merge --no-ff    SKIPPED             SKIPPED
#
# The twice-pulled send this tree runs REQUIRES a rebase whenever the anointed remote moved
# (`construction/ITINERARY.md`, the sync rota), so the third and fourth rows are not exotic paths
# -- they are the ordinary close of a contested round. On 20260829 a round landed through a
# cherry-picked re-derivation, and BOTH pages shipped stale in a commit that added a witness. The
# tree was clean, every witness the round re-ran was green, and the staleness surfaced at the NEXT
# lap's cold roster as two red rows in somebody else's round (REDS %337).
#
# HOW IT KNOWS, and why it asks this question rather than another. The obvious reading is to detect
# the sequencer -- probe for .git/rebase-merge, .git/rebase-apply, .git/sequencer, CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
# -- and that is an inference from four situations to the property actually wanted. This asks the
# property directly: DID MY SIBLING RUN? pre-commit drops a token in the git directory on its
# success path, and this hook reads it. A path git adds tomorrow, or one nobody thought of, answers
# correctly on the first run without anyone editing a list of four names.
#
# BOTH MARKERS LIVE INSIDE THE GIT DIRECTORY, never in the work tree, and that is the safety
# property: a rebase refuses to continue over unstaged changes, so a hook that wrote into the tree
# mid-replay could wedge the very rebase it was helping. These files are invisible to `git status`.
#
# A SECOND DEBT JOINED THE FIRST on 20260906 (REDS 20260906.211529). pre-commit RULE THREE refuses
# a commit staging a living pin that is over the byte bound its own header declares, and it sits on
# the same skipped path as rule one -- so a pin can cross its bound inside a rebase-replayed commit
# and no wall reads it. Measured in a pen with both hooks armed: a fat pin landed by `git rebase`
# stands at 150 bytes against a bound of 100, `derived-pages-owed` IS recorded, and the next
# ordinary commit passes free. Six of the last 120 commits touching construction/REDS.md shipped it
# over its declared bound that way. So this hook records a pin debt beside the page debt, and
# pre-commit pays it by weighing EVERY rostered pin off the index rather than only the staged ones.
#
# WHY THE PIN DEBT IS RECORDED HERE AND NOT MEASURED HERE, which is rule one's reasoning reused
# rather than restated: this hook runs once per replayed commit, and a rebase of ten commits would
# pay ten whole-roster readings to answer a question the next commit answers once. Recording costs
# one `: >`.
#
# WHAT THE SECOND DEBT CANNOT DO, said plainly: it refuses the NEXT commit, so a rebase followed
# immediately by a push ships the over-bound pin once. The tree's own send ritual puts a
# `git commit --amend` between the two -- the Git nib is rewritten to HEAD's parent after the final
# rebase -- and git runs pre-commit for an amend, so on the ordinary send path the debt is paid
# before anything is public. A send that rebases and pushes with no amend is the gap, and it is
# named rather than closed here, because a pre-push wall is a bigger blast radius than a
# pre-commit one and wants its own lap.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. Two file tests and at most one `: >` -- microseconds. Rendering here was weighed
# and declined: the two generators take 3.1s and 9.4s measured on this bench 20260829, and a rebase
# replaying ten commits would pay two minutes to answer a question the next commit answers free.
# So the debt is RECORDED here and PAID by the next pre-commit, which already knows how to refresh
# a page and how to behave about the author's own unstaged edits.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT REACH, said plainly. `git merge --no-ff` creates a commit and runs neither
# hook, so a page staled by one goes unseen here. This tree's seated send merges `--ff-only`, which
# creates no commit at all and stales nothing, so the gap sits off the path the tree walks -- named
# rather than closed, because a `post-merge` hook would also fire on every `git pull` fast-forward
# and mark a debt no local commit incurred. The rostered guards remain the backstop for both.
#
# Proven both ways by tools/g/generated_page_freshness_witness.rish over
# tools/fixtures/g/generated_page_freshness_control.sh.
# Armed by rishi/bin/rishi run tools/i/install_hooks.rish.

set -eu

# The same first gate pre-commit keeps: a tree without rishi -- the depersonalized seed, a shallow
# checkout -- carries neither generator, so it can owe no page. Without this the seed's own root
# commit would mark a debt nothing in it could pay.
[ -x rishi/bin/rishi ] || exit 0

gitdir=$(git rev-parse --git-dir)
ran="$gitdir/pre-commit-ran"
owed="$gitdir/derived-pages-owed"
pins_owed="$gitdir/living-pins-owed"

if [ -f "$ran" ]; then
  # The commit that just landed ran pre-commit, so both rules had their say -- including the case
  # where either fired BECAUSE a debt was standing. A debt is paid by the landing, never by the
  # intent.
  rm -f "$ran" "$owed" "$pins_owed"
  exit 0
fi

: > "$owed"
: > "$pins_owed"
echo "post-commit: this commit did not run pre-commit -- a rebase, a cherry-pick, or a path like"
echo "  them. Two debts are recorded for the next commit here to pay."
echo "  The two generated pages were not refreshed with it -- refresh them now, or let the next"
echo "  commit do it:"
echo "    rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/readme_metrics.rish write"
echo "    rishi/bin/rishi run tools/g/geode_libraries.rish write"
echo "  And no living pin was weighed. Read them all now, or let the next commit refuse if one is"
echo "  over the byte bound its own header declares:"
echo "    sh tools/fixtures/p/pin_bound_touch_scan.sh index"
exit 0
